How can cyclic voltammetry (CV) detect an EC reaction, where an electron-transfer (E) step is followed by a chemical (C) reaction?
</USER_REQUEST>

<GROUND_TRUTH>
In cyclic voltammetry, the forward peak current (\(I_{pa}\)) corresponds to the oxidation of the reactant (e.g., bromide to bromine), while the reverse peak current (\(I_{pc}\)) corresponds to the reduction of the product back to the reactant. For an EC reaction, the chemical step consumes some of the product (e.g., bromine reacts with allyl alcohol), reducing its concentration. This results in \(I_{pc}\) being smaller than \(I_{pa}\), indicating the occurrence of the chemical reaction following electron transfer. Thus, a deviation in the peak current ratio (\(I_{pc}/I_{pa} < 1\)) is evidence of an EC reaction. 

\boxed{I_{pc}/I_{pa} < 1 \text{ indicates an EC reaction.}}
</GROUND_TRUTH>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
