","To calculate the work done, we use the formula:

\[W = F \cdot d \cdot \cos(\theta)\]

Where:
- \( W \) is the work done (in seconds)
- \( F \) is the force applied (in newtons)
- \( d \) is the distance covered (in meters)
- \( \theta \) is the angle between the force vector and the displacement vector (in degrees)

Given:
- \( F = 60.0 \text{N} \)
- \( d = 10.0 \text{m} \)
- \( \theta = 60.0^\circ \)

Substituting the values into the formula:

\[ W = 60.0 \text{N} \cdot 10.0 \text{m} \cdot \cos(60.0^\circ) \]

Since \( \cos(60^\circ) = 0.5 \):

\[ W = 60.0 \text{N} \cdot 10.0 \text{m} \cdot 0.5 \]
\[ W = 300 \text{ N m} \]

