\[
\mathbf{V}(x, y) = P(x, y)\mathbf{i} + Q(x, y)\mathbf{j} + R(x, y)\mathbf{k}
\]
\[
\mathbf{V} = P\mathbf{i} + Q\mathbf{j} + R\mathbf{k}
\]
The curl of \(\mathbf{V}\) is given by:
\[
\mathbf{V} \times \nabla = \left(\frac{\partial R}{\partial y} - \frac{\partial Q}{\partial z}\right)\mathbf{i} - \left(\frac{\partial R}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial P}{\partial z}\right)\mathbf{j} + \left(\frac{\partial P}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial Q}{\partial x}\right)\mathbf{k}
\]
where \(\nabla = \mathbf{i}\frac{\partial}{\partial x} + \mathbf{J}\frac{\partial}{\partial y} + \mathbf{k}\frac{\partial}{\partial z}\).

Given that \(\mathbf{V}\) only depends on \(x\) and \(y\), we can express it as:
\[
\mathbf{V}(x, y) = P(x, y)\mathbf{i} + Q(x, y)\mathbf{j} + 0\mathbf{k}
\]
Substituting into the formula for the curl:
\[
\mathbf{V} \times \mathbf{k} = \left(\frac{\partial 0}{\partial y} - \frac{\partial Q}{\partial z}\right)\mathbf{i} - \left(\frac{\partial 0}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial P}{\partial z}\right)\mathbf{j} + \left(\frac{\partial P}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial Q}{\partial x}\right)\mathbf{k}
\]
Since \(R = 0\) and the vector field only satisfies \(x\) and \(y\), the derivatives with \(z\) are zero. Thus, the expression simplifies to:
\[
\boxed{\left(\frac{\partial P}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial Q}{\partial y}\right)\mathbf{k}}
