- \( E_p = \hbar \omega_p \) is the plasmon energy,
- \( e \) is the elementary charge,
- \( \hbar \) is the reduced Planck constant,
- \( \omega_p \) is the plasmon frequency.

This formula is valid when the primary electron energy \( E_0 \) is much greater than the Fermi energy \( E_F \). 

The cross section describes the probability of plasmon excitation due to the interaction of low-energy electrons with the solid. 

Final answer: \(\boxed{\sigma_p = \frac{e^2 \omega_p}{\hbar} \ln \left[ \frac{(E_F + E_p)^{1/2} - E_p^{1/2}}{E_0^{1/2} - (E_0 - E_p)^{1/2}} \right]}\).
